{"topicSections":[{"identifiers":["doc:\/\/ImplicitGraphs\/documentation\/ImplicitGraphs\/ImplicitDirectedGraph\/OutEdges","doc:\/\/ImplicitGraphs\/documentation\/ImplicitGraphs\/ImplicitDirectedGraph\/Successors"],"anchor":"Structures","title":"Structures","generated":true},{"identifiers":["doc:\/\/ImplicitGraphs\/documentation\/ImplicitGraphs\/ImplicitDirectedGraph\/init(vertexCount:successors:)"],"anchor":"Initializers","title":"Initializers","generated":true},{"identifiers":["doc:\/\/ImplicitGraphs\/documentation\/ImplicitGraphs\/ImplicitDirectedGraph\/DirectedGraph-Implementations"],"anchor":"Default-Implementations","title":"Default Implementations","generated":true}],"identifier":{"interfaceLanguage":"swift","url":"doc:\/\/ImplicitGraphs\/documentation\/ImplicitGraphs\/ImplicitDirectedGraph"},"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/ImplicitGraphs\/documentation\/ImplicitGraphs"]]},"kind":"symbol","metadata":{"symbolKind":"struct","remoteSource":{"fileName":"ImplicitDirectedGraph.swift","url":"https:\/\/github.com\/Tyler-Keith-Thompson\/Grafluent\/blob\/main\/Sources\/ImplicitGraphs\/ImplicitDirectedGraph.swift#L38"},"roleHeading":"Structure","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"struct"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"ImplicitDirectedGraph"}],"navigatorTitle":[{"text":"ImplicitDirectedGraph","kind":"identifier"}],"modules":[{"name":"ImplicitGraphs"}],"title":"ImplicitDirectedGraph","externalID":"s:14ImplicitGraphs0A13DirectedGraphV","role":"symbol"},"schemaVersion":{"minor":3,"patch":0,"major":0},"sections":[],"abstract":[{"text":"A finite directed graph on the vertices ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"0..<vertexCount"},{"text":" whose out-neighbors come from a","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"closure, called on demand and never stored: an implicit graph (Boost’s ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"grid_graph"},{"text":", LEMON’s","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"GridGraph"},{"text":" and ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"FullGraph"},{"text":"). It is a lawful ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"DirectedGraph"},{"text":", so every generic algorithm runs","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"on it, including those that need the whole vertex set (components, centrality), with no edge","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"storage.","type":"text"}],"relationshipsSections":[{"kind":"relationships","identifiers":["doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/DirectedGraph","doc:\/\/ImplicitGraphs\/s8CopyableP","doc:\/\/ImplicitGraphs\/s9EscapableP"],"type":"conformsTo","title":"Conforms To"}],"primaryContentSections":[{"kind":"declarations","declarations":[{"tokens":[{"kind":"attribute","text":"@frozen"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"keyword","text":"struct"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"ImplicitDirectedGraph"},{"kind":"text","text":"<"},{"kind":"genericParameter","text":"Row"},{"kind":"text","text":"> "},{"kind":"keyword","text":"where"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"typeIdentifier","text":"Row"},{"kind":"text","text":" : "},{"preciseIdentifier":"s:ST","kind":"typeIdentifier","text":"Sequence"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"kind":"typeIdentifier","text":"Row"},{"kind":"text","text":"."},{"preciseIdentifier":"s:ST7ElementQa","kind":"typeIdentifier","text":"Element"},{"kind":"text","text":" == "},{"preciseIdentifier":"s:Si","kind":"typeIdentifier","text":"Int"}],"platforms":["Linux"],"languages":["swift"]}]},{"kind":"content","content":[{"type":"heading","text":"Overview","level":2,"anchor":"overview"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"For a state space with no vertex collection (an unbounded grid, a puzzle), use the closure","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"searches instead: Traversal’s ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"breadthFirstSearch(from:successors:)"},{"text":" and ShortestPaths’","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"dijkstraShortestPath(from:cutoff:successors:success:)"},{"text":" and ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"aStarShortestPath(from:cutoff: successors:heuristic:success:)"},{"text":". For ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Hashable"},{"text":" states over a finite set, number them and use","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"LabeledGraph<State, ImplicitDirectedGraph<Row>>"},{"text":"; a grid is numbered row-major.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"strong","inlineContent":[{"text":"The closure.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"successors(v)"},{"type":"text","text":" lists the targets of the edges leaving "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"v"},{"type":"text","text":", once per edge, as"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"any "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Sequence<Int>"},{"type":"text","text":" (an array, a lazy filter over a fixed neighbor list), so a specialized build"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"inlines it with no type erasure. Each read of a row calls it once: "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"successors(of:)"},{"type":"text","text":","},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"outEdges(of:)"},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"outDegree(of:)"},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"contains(edge:)"},{"type":"text","text":" once each, "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"edges"},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"edgeCount"},{"type":"text","text":" once"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"per vertex. It must return the same sequence each time it is asked about the same vertex (the"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"protocol’s “asking again gives the same answer”); this is documented, not checked, and a"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"closure that breaks it breaks every algorithm, as a mutating "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"=="},{"type":"text","text":" breaks "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Set"},{"type":"text","text":". Each target is"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"checked to be a vertex when it is read, not when the row is formed."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"On a hot path, return a small struct that computes the row up front into fixed slots and"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"yields them by count, rather than a "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"lazy.filter"},{"type":"text","text":" over a fixed neighbor list: the filter calls"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"its predicate per element and keeps the unfiltered iterator’s state, and in the benchmarks on a"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"1000 × 1000 grid it cost 1.2–1.8× the precomputed row (breadth-first layers, components,"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"Dijkstra)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Positions.","type":"text"}],"type":"strong"},{"text":" An edge’s position is ","type":"text"},{"code":"Edges.Index","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":": its source, its rank in the source’s row,","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"and its target. Equality, order and hashing use ","type":"text"},{"code":"(source, rank)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", unique even among parallel","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"edges and self-loops; the target rides along, so ","type":"text"},{"code":"source(ofEdgeAt:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"code":"target(ofEdgeAt:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" and a","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"weight closure reading ","type":"text"},{"code":"$0.target","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" call nothing. There are no dense edge indices.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Not "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Equatable"},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Hashable"},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Codable"},{"type":"text","text":" or "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Sendable"},{"type":"text","text":": a closure has no identity and is not"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"@Sendable"},{"type":"text","text":"."}]}]}],"variants":[{"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}],"paths":["\/documentation\/implicitgraphs\/implicitdirectedgraph"]}],"references":{"doc://ImplicitGraphs/s9EscapableP":{"identifier":"doc:\/\/ImplicitGraphs\/s9EscapableP","type":"unresolvable","title":"Swift.Escapable"},"doc://ImplicitGraphs/documentation/ImplicitGraphs":{"identifier":"doc:\/\/ImplicitGraphs\/documentation\/ImplicitGraphs","type":"topic","abstract":[],"role":"collection","title":"ImplicitGraphs","url":"\/documentation\/implicitgraphs","kind":"symbol"},"doc://ImplicitGraphs/documentation/ImplicitGraphs/ImplicitDirectedGraph":{"identifier":"doc:\/\/ImplicitGraphs\/documentation\/ImplicitGraphs\/ImplicitDirectedGraph","url":"\/documentation\/implicitgraphs\/implicitdirectedgraph","role":"symbol","abstract":[{"text":"A finite directed graph on the vertices ","type":"text"},{"code":"0..<vertexCount","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" whose out-neighbors come from a","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"closure, called on demand and never stored: an implicit graph (Boost’s ","type":"text"},{"code":"grid_graph","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", LEMON’s","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"code":"GridGraph","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" and ","type":"text"},{"code":"FullGraph","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"). It is a lawful ","type":"text"},{"code":"DirectedGraph","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", so every generic algorithm runs","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"on it, including those that need the whole vertex set (components, centrality), with no edge","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"storage.","type":"text"}],"title":"ImplicitDirectedGraph","navigatorTitle":[{"text":"ImplicitDirectedGraph","kind":"identifier"}],"kind":"symbol","type":"topic","fragments":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"ImplicitDirectedGraph","kind":"identifier"}]},"doc://GraphProtocols/documentation/GraphProtocols/DirectedGraph":{"url":"\/documentation\/graphprotocols\/directedgraph","kind":"symbol","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"protocol"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"DirectedGraph"}],"abstract":[{"text":"A directed graph: a finite set of vertices and a finite collection of directed edges between","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"them.","type":"text"}],"type":"topic","title":"DirectedGraph","identifier":"doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/DirectedGraph","role":"symbol","navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"DirectedGraph"}]},"doc://ImplicitGraphs/documentation/ImplicitGraphs/ImplicitDirectedGraph/DirectedGraph-Implementations":{"identifier":"doc:\/\/ImplicitGraphs\/documentation\/ImplicitGraphs\/ImplicitDirectedGraph\/DirectedGraph-Implementations","type":"topic","title":"DirectedGraph Implementations","url":"\/documentation\/implicitgraphs\/implicitdirectedgraph\/directedgraph-implementations","kind":"article","role":"collectionGroup","abstract":[]},"doc://ImplicitGraphs/documentation/ImplicitGraphs/ImplicitDirectedGraph/Successors":{"kind":"symbol","url":"\/documentation\/implicitgraphs\/implicitdirectedgraph\/successors","identifier":"doc:\/\/ImplicitGraphs\/documentation\/ImplicitGraphs\/ImplicitDirectedGraph\/Successors","abstract":[{"text":"The closure’s row for one vertex, each target checked to be a vertex as it is read (not","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"when the row is formed). A struct rather than a lazy map, so an algorithm that keeps an","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text